WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.299
Uninstalling shouldn't mean losing every conversation and setting you've built up.

2
00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:07.000
Here's the clean uninstall path, and exactly where the “actually delete everything” line is.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.090
Uninstall the normal way for your platform: through Windows Settings, or dpkg minus r on Linux.

4
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.000
Standard uninstall, nothing special to remember on this step.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.714
Here's the deliberate part: the ~/.

6
00:00:18.714 --> 00:00:20.233
harnessx folder is left behind.

7
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.906
That's on purpose — it's your conversations, your keys, your configuration.

8
00:00:23.906 --> 00:00:26.404
An uninstall removes the program, not your history.

9
00:00:26.404 --> 00:00:29.000
If you reinstall later, it's all still there waiting.

10
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.479
Want everything gone?

11
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:31.183
Delete ~/.

12
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:32.380
harnessx by hand.

13
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:39.000
That's the actual line — the installer never touches it, so removing it is entirely your call.

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.089
If you're backing that folder up instead, know what's in it: models and engines are the large folders, and they're entirely re-downloadable.

15
00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:51.000
Excluding them from a backup saves gigabytes without losing anything that can't be fetched again.

16
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Speaking of backups — next episode is the real walkthrough: compressing that folder, moving it to a new machine, and what to watch for when you get there.

